---
import DocsLayout from "../../layouts/DocsLayout.astro";
import CodeBlock from "../../components/docs/CodeBlock.astro";
import { docsNav } from "../../data/docs-nav";

const toc = [
  { id: "what-it-does", label: "What Aegis does" },
  { id: "how-a-scan-works", label: "How a scan works" },
  { id: "install-it", label: "Install it" },
  { id: "documentation-map", label: "Documentation map" },
  { id: "conventions", label: "Conventions" },
];
---

<DocsLayout
  title="Aegis documentation"
  description="Aegis reviews a repository, derives its attack surface, tests that surface, and reports only the findings it could verify."
  toc={toc}
>
  <h2 id="what-it-does">What Aegis does</h2>
  <p>
    Aegis is a command-line tool that runs on a checkout of your repository.
    It reads code changes, data flows, and dependencies, generates security
    tests against the entry points it finds, executes those tests, and reports
    the findings that it was able to reproduce.
  </p>
  <p>
    Everything runs locally by default. No source code leaves the machine unless
    you explicitly configure a remote ruleset or reporting destination.
  </p>

  <h2 id="how-a-scan-works">How a scan works</h2>
  <ol>
    <li>
      <strong>Review.</strong> Aegis parses the working tree (or a diff
      range) and builds a model of routes, handlers, queries, and dependencies.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Map.</strong> Entry points and data flows are resolved into an
      attack surface — the set of places untrusted input can reach sensitive
      operations.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Test.</strong> Targeted tests are generated per candidate finding
      and executed against that surface.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Report.</strong> Findings are emitted with a severity, a source
      location, the evidence that proved them, and a remediation.
    </li>
  </ol>

  <h2 id="install-it">Install it</h2>
  <CodeBlock title="shell">curl -fsSL aegis.dev/install | sh
aegis --version</CodeBlock>
  <p>
    See <a href="/docs/install/">Install</a> for package managers, pinned
    versions, and offline installation.
  </p>

  <h2 id="documentation-map">Documentation map</h2>
  <table>
    <thead>
      <tr><th>Section</th><th>Contents</th></tr>
    </thead>
    <tbody>
      {docsNav.map((section) => (
        <tr>
          <td><strong>{section.title}</strong></td>
          <td>
            {section.links.map((link, i) => (
              <>
                {i > 0 && ", "}
                <a href={link.href}>{link.label}</a>
              </>
            ))}
          </td>
        </tr>
      ))}
    </tbody>
  </table>

  <h2 id="conventions">Conventions</h2>
  <p>Throughout this documentation:</p>
  <ul>
    <li>Text in <code>code font</code> is typed literally.</li>
    <li>
      Text in <code>ITALIC_CAPS</code> is a placeholder you replace, such as
      <code>PATH</code> or <code>FORMAT</code>.
    </li>
    <li>
      Square brackets mark optional arguments: <code>aegis scan [PATH]</code>.
    </li>
    <li>
      A vertical bar separates alternatives:
      <code>--format=json|sarif|text</code>.
    </li>
    <li>An ellipsis marks a repeatable argument: <code>PATH ...</code>.</li>
  </ul>
</DocsLayout>
